dwmapi: implement the DwmGetWindowAttribute attributes Windows answers, and store
        DwmSetWindowAttribute values

Symptom.  Running Solid Edge 2026 under Wine, clicking "Close Sketch" printed
    06cc:fixme:dwmapi:DwmGetWindowAttribute attribute 14 not implemented in the terminal
and the sketch never closed.  Attribute 14 is DWMWA_CLOAKED.  Wine's
DwmGetWindowAttribute implemented DWMWA_EXTENDED_FRAME_BOUNDS and answered every other
attribute with FIXME + E_NOTIMPL; DwmSetWindowAttribute was a stub that returned S_OK
without storing anything, so no attribute could round-trip.

Who reads it.  The caller is not Solid Edge's own code: it is .NET Framework's
UIAutomationClient.dll, which Wine loads because the Solid Edge UI calls into UI
Automation.  Disassembled with ILSpy (tools/dumper):

    private static bool IsWindowCloaked(HWND hwnd)
    {
        int pvAttributeValue = 0;
        if (SafeNativeMethods.DwmGetWindowAttribute(hwnd, 14, ref pvAttributeValue, 4) == 0)
            return pvAttributeValue != 0;
        return false;
    }
    private static bool IsWindowReallyVisible(HWND hwnd)
    {
        if (!IsWindowVisible(hwnd)) return false;
        if (!Misc.GetWindowRect(hwnd, out var rc)) return false;
        if (rc.right - rc.left <= 0 || rc.bottom - rc.top <= 0) return false;
        if (IsWindowCloaked(hwnd)) return false;
        return true;
    }

so the FIXME is not merely noise: it is Wine answering a question about a window with an
HRESULT Windows never returns there.  Any caller that switches on the HRESULT (rather than
treating "anything but S_OK" as one case) sees a Windows-unreachable branch.

The Windows behaviour restored.  Measured, not read, with tools/winapi/dwmprobe.c -- one PE
run on the Windows 11 guest and under Wine (Windows column below is the guest):

    attr  1 DWMWA_NCRENDERING_ENABLED            S_OK      DWORD 1
    attr  5 DWMWA_CAPTION_BUTTON_BOUNDS          S_OK      RECT ; size<16 -> 0x8007007a
    attr  9 DWMWA_EXTENDED_FRAME_BOUNDS           S_OK      RECT ; size<16 -> 0x8007007a
                                                           child window -> E_HANDLE
    attr 14 DWMWA_CLOAKED                        S_OK      DWORD 0
    attr 15 DWMWA_FREEZE_REPRESENTATION          S_OK      DWORD 0
    attr 16 DWMWA_PASSIVE_UPDATE_MODE            S_OK      DWORD 0
    attr 19/33 DWMWA_WINDOW_CORNER_PREFERENCE     S_OK      DWORD 0
    attr 20 DWMWA_USE_IMMERSIVE_DARK_MODE        S_OK      DWORD 0
    attr 37 DWMWA_VISIBLE_FRAME_BORDER_THICKNESS  S_OK      DWORD 1, size must be exactly 4
    attr 38 DWMWA_SYSTEMBACKDROP_TYPE            S_OK      DWORD 0
    everything else (0,2,3,4,6..13,17,18,21..32,34,35,36,39)  E_INVALIDARG
    attr 14 with pv=NULL            -> E_INVALIDARG
    attr 14 with size 0/1/3         -> E_INVALIDARG   (the DWORD attributes want >= 4)
    attr 14 with a bogus hwnd       -> E_HANDLE
    set 20 then get 20              -> 1
    set 33 then get 33              -> 2
    set 34 (BORDER_COLOR) -> S_OK, get 34 -> E_INVALIDARG

So three things changed: the attributes Windows answers are answered with those values and
sizes, unknown attributes are E_INVALIDARG (**not** E_NOTIMPL), and the values an application
sets are kept so DwmGetWindowAttribute returns them again.  Storage is a window property, so
it lives and dies with the window.

Verification.  dlls/dwmapi/tests/dwmapi.c gains test_DWMWA_attributes(), which asserts the
whole table above (skipping the Windows-8-and-later attributes when the platform rejects
them, as Windows 7 does).  Run:

    tools/run_wine_tests.sh dwmapi dwmapi        # 2 tests, 0 failures

and the probe's two outputs diff clean against the guest's.
